[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: DNS design/configuration on the central resolver (BIND/named).
Mechanism: Your internal zone is named “local”, which collides with the special-use .local domain reserved for Multicast DNS (mDNS). Many client OSes first try mDNS for *.local names; across routed/VLAN’d networks mDNS won’t reach the DNS server, so the resolver waits on mDNS timeouts before falling back to unicast DNS. Sometimes the answer is already cached so it’s instant; other times it “spins” while mDNS times out, then succeeds.
Localization: On dns_server, BIND is authoritative for zone "local" (see /etc/bind/named.conf and /etc/bind/db.local with web0/web1/web2/web3 A records). The server is reachable and answers authoritatively for web1.local (dig from a host returns NOERROR with A 10.200.0.4), but note dig’s warning that “.local is reserved for Multicast DNS.” Clients point at 10.200.0.2 as their sole nameserver (/etc/resolv.conf). We also observed recursion disabled on the server (recursion no;), so it refuses non-local queries (dig example.com returns REFUSED), which can add further page load delays for sites that pull external assets.
Root cause: The internal DNS zone uses the .local TLD, conflicting with mDNS behavior on clients; additionally, recursion is disabled, causing REFUSED for external lookups.
Corrective fix: Rename the internal zone to a non-.local domain (e.g., corp.internal or a subdomain of your real domain) and update records/clients’ search suffix. Optionally enable safe recursion/forwarders on dns_server for external resolution. This will eliminate mDNS contention and the intermittent delays. 
=== END ===